<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<ContentPage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui" xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml" xmlns:vm="clr-namespace:CafePosApp.ViewModels" xmlns:shapes="clr-namespace:Microsoft.Maui.Controls.Shapes;assembly=Microsoft.Maui.Controls" x:Class="CafePosApp.Views.OrderDetailsPage" x:DataType="vm:OrderDetailsViewModel" Title="Детали заказа"
             Shell.NavBarIsVisible="True"><!-- Explicit, not inherited: this page is pushed from
             OrdersPage, which sets the property False to drop its duplicate tab title, and the
             back arrow on this page depends on the bar being there. Verified on the emulator. -->
    <!-- Five rows, not four: the header, the message, the fulfilment/contact/promise card, the line
         list (the only `*` row), and the action block. The new card is its own Auto row rather than
         packed into the header so the header stays two lines on a narrow window and the card can be
         hidden entirely while there is no order loaded. -->
    <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto,Auto,*,Auto" Padding="16" RowSpacing="10">
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">
            <VerticalStackLayout>
                <Label Text="{Binding OrderTitle}" FontSize="22" FontAttributes="Bold" />
                <Label Text="{Binding StatusText}" Style="{StaticResource SecondaryLabel}" />
            </VerticalStackLayout>
            <ActivityIndicator Grid.Column="1" IsRunning="{Binding IsBusy}" IsVisible="{Binding IsBusy}" />
        </Grid>
        <!-- The value here is static — every message this ViewModel raises is a failure — so an
             AppThemeBinding on the attribute is the whole fix and needs no ViewModel change. A bare
             {StaticResource Danger} declared the light token in both themes: on the page fill
             (#FAFAFA / #121212, this page has no CardBorder) that is 4.77:1 in light and 3.76:1 in
             dark, and at 14pt regular 3.76:1 fails the 4.5:1 it owes. DangerDark #FF9E8E on #121212
             is 9.40:1. -->
        <Label Grid.Row="1" Text="{Binding Message}" IsVisible="{Binding HasMessage}"
               TextColor="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource Danger}, Dark={StaticResource DangerDark}}"
               LineBreakMode="WordWrap" />

        <!-- ═══ FULFILMENT, CONTACT AND PROMISE — ONE ROW, EXPANDING IN PLACE ═══
             READ-ONLY, and that is a KNOWN LIMITATION rather than a design choice: the till collects
             these three on the cart and CheckoutService writes them onto the order, but
             IOrderService.UpdateOrderAsync takes only the item list, so there is no write path back
             from here. Offering a control that looks editable and is not would be worse than showing
             the facts — see OrderDetailsViewModel for the Core change that would close it.

             THE SAME DISCLOSURE THE CART USES, so the two order-entry screens read as one design
             rather than as a dense screen and a loose one. It costs more here than on the cart, and
             the cost is stated on OrderDetailsViewModel.IsFulfilmentExpanded: the number below is the
             FULL one and this is the screen a cashier dials from, so the collapse puts one tap
             between the operator and it. What it saves is two short lines.

             Two things inside are deliberately NOT what the customer sees. The phone is in FULL,
             because a masked number cannot be verified or used and the cart and the board both mask
             it already — those two stand in the room. And the promise keeps its EXACT time AND its
             qualifier, «к 18:00» for a time the customer named against «обещано к 18:00» for one the
             till estimated: the collapsed row above has room for the time and not for the word that
             says which of the two it is, so the collapse costs a qualifier and not a fact.

             WHILE OPEN, THE ROW SAYS ONLY «Параметры заказа», for the same reason the cart's row
             switches to «Параметры выдачи»: directly beneath it sit «Выдача: В зале» and «Готово …」
             restating the very facts the collapsed row carries, so printing them in the row as well
             would say each one twice within two rows. Both captions come from ONE property computed
             off IsFulfilmentExpanded rather than from two strings kept in step by hand — see
             OrderDetailsViewModel.FulfilmentSummary, and MenuViewModel.FulfilmentSummary for the cart
             half of the same rule.

             No IsEnabled on any TapGestureRecognizer on this page: a recognizer derives from
             GestureRecognizer : Element, is not a VisualElement and has no such property — writing it
             threw XamlParseException the first time a line template was realised. Conditional
             interactivity comes from the parent's IsVisible or from Command.CanExecute, and the
             NotifyCanExecuteChanged() that re-runs it is at OrderDetailsViewModel's NotifyOrderState. -->
        <VerticalStackLayout Grid.Row="2" Spacing="4" IsVisible="{Binding HasOrderDetails}">
            <Border MinimumHeightRequest="48" Padding="8,2" StrokeShape="RoundRectangle 8"
                    BackgroundColor="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource SurfaceVariant}, Dark={StaticResource SurfaceVariantDark}}">
                <Border.GestureRecognizers>
                    <!-- On the ROW Border only. The expanded block below is its SIBLING and not its
                         child, so a tap on the phone or on the lateness line never collapses the block
                         out from under the finger that is reaching for it. -->
                    <TapGestureRecognizer Command="{Binding Source={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type ContentPage}}, Path=BindingContext.ToggleFulfilmentCommand}" />
                </Border.GestureRecognizers>
                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8">
                    <Label Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding FulfilmentSummary}" FontAttributes="Bold" FontSize="14"
                           VerticalOptions="Center" LineBreakMode="TailTruncation"
                           SemanticProperties.Description="{Binding FulfilmentToggleHint}" />
                    <!-- A literal Geometry in both triggers, and only Data differs. A trigger's Setter
                         takes a VALUE and not a binding expression, so an {AppThemeBinding} inside one
                         resolves against the light theme at parse time and never follows a theme change
                         — the MenuPage message-colour defect in miniature. The stroke is an attribute,
                         where it does follow. Not a text glyph either: the implicit style sets
                         FontFamily=OpenSansRegular, which has no «⌄». -->
                    <shapes:Path Grid.Column="1" Aspect="Uniform" WidthRequest="16" HeightRequest="16"
                                 VerticalOptions="Center" HorizontalOptions="End"
                                 Stroke="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource Gray700}, Dark={StaticResource Gray300}}"
                                 StrokeThickness="2" Fill="Transparent"
                                 Data="M4,2 L14,8 L4,14">
                        <shapes:Path.Triggers>
                            <DataTrigger TargetType="shapes:Path" Binding="{Binding IsFulfilmentExpanded}" Value="True">
                                <Setter Property="Data" Value="M2,5 L8,11 M14,5 L8,11" />
                            </DataTrigger>
                        </shapes:Path.Triggers>
                    </shapes:Path>
                </Grid>
            </Border>

            <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="2" IsVisible="{Binding IsFulfilmentExpanded}">
                <HorizontalStackLayout Spacing="8">
                    <Label Text="{Binding OrderTypeText, StringFormat='Выдача: {0}'}" Style="{StaticResource SecondaryLabel}" FontAttributes="Bold" />
                    <Label Text="{Binding PromiseText, StringFormat='Готово {0}'}" Style="{StaticResource SecondaryLabel}" FontAttributes="Bold" />
                </HorizontalStackLayout>
                <!-- Present only for a takeaway order. Counter service stores no phone at all, so the
                     row would be a masked nothing; hiding it makes that honest. The counter case says
                     so in words rather than leaving a blank a reader has to interpret — «телефон не
                     спрашивали» is a different fact from «телефон забыли», and only one of them is
                     correct for an order eaten on the premises. -->
                <Label Text="{Binding CustomerPhoneText, StringFormat='Телефон: {0}'}" Style="{StaticResource SecondaryLabel}"
                       IsVisible="{Binding HasCustomerPhone}"
                       SemanticProperties.Description="Телефон клиента, полностью" />
                <Label Text="Телефон не спрашивали — заказ в зале." Style="{StaticResource TagText}" FontSize="11"
                       IsVisible="{Binding ExpectsNoPhone}" />
                <Label Text="{Binding OverdueText}" TextColor="{Binding OverdueColor}" FontSize="12" FontAttributes="Bold"
                       IsVisible="{Binding IsOverdue}" />
            </VerticalStackLayout>
        </VerticalStackLayout>

        <CollectionView Grid.Row="3" ItemsSource="{Binding Items}" SelectionMode="None">
            <CollectionView.EmptyView>
                <Label Text="В заказе нет блюд." Style="{StaticResource EmptyLabel}" />
            </CollectionView.EmptyView>
            <CollectionView.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:OrderEditItemViewModel">
                    <!-- THE SAME LINE AS THE CART: name and price on one row, quantity beside them,
                         the line total dropped. LineTotalText was «220,00 ₿» under a «Цена:
                         220,00 ₿» — the same figure twice at quantity 1, and the first multiplied by
                         a number already on screen at any other quantity. The order total at the foot
                         of the page is Σ over these lines and is unchanged, so nothing is lost but
                         the duplication. The variant and modifier keep a line each, because on a
                         411dp screen they get a line each.

                         THE DECORATION MATCHES THE CART: «(модификатор)» and «[вариант]», not
                         «Модификатор: …» / «Вариант: …». Measured on the emulator, the name column
                         on this row is ~115dp once the 48dp stepper, the 48dp «Удалить» and the
                         price have taken theirs, and «Модификатор: Обычное» truncated to
                         «Модификатор: Обы…» at that width. Dropping the two label words buys the
                         ~150px that made the row readable. Keeping «Удалить» is not negotiable —
                         stepping down to zero is a quantity edit, removing the line is a deletion —
                         so the name is what gives. And matching the cart's brackets and
                         parentheses is what makes the two order-entry screens one design instead
                         of two. -->
                    <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto,Auto,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8" Padding="0,2">
                        <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="0" VerticalOptions="Center">
                            <Label Text="{Binding ProductName}" FontAttributes="Bold" LineBreakMode="TailTruncation" />
                            <Label Text="{Binding SelectedModifierName, StringFormat='({0})'}" Style="{StaticResource SecondaryLabel}" FontSize="11" LineBreakMode="TailTruncation" IsVisible="{Binding SelectedModifierName, Converter={StaticResource IsPresent}}" />
                            <Label Text="{Binding SelectedVariantName, StringFormat='[{0}]'}" Style="{StaticResource SecondaryLabel}" FontSize="11" LineBreakMode="TailTruncation" IsVisible="{Binding SelectedVariantName, Converter={StaticResource IsPresent}}" />

                            <!-- The composition, indented under the line. A bundle that prints as one
                                 line is a bundle the kitchen cannot make, and this is a STAFF screen
                                 preparing one — so the slots are on the row, and tapping them opens the
                                 same composition sheet the cart opens. The tap exists only for a bundle
                                 because this block is bound to IsVisible: on an ordinary dish it is out
                                 of the tree.

                                 NO IsEnabled ON THE RECOGNIZER. TapGestureRecognizer derives from
                                 GestureRecognizer : Element — it is not a VisualElement and has no
                                 IsEnabled — and the attribute that used to sit here threw
                                 XamlParseException the first time a row was realised, i.e. inside the
                                 load, behind a page-level catch that turned it into «не удалось
                                 загрузить» under figures that had already updated. Its liveness now
                                 comes from Command.CanExecute, which the IL of SendTapped checks
                                 before Execute; see OrderDetailsViewModel for the CanExecute wiring. -->
                            <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="0" Padding="12,0,0,0" IsVisible="{Binding IsCombo}">
                                <VerticalStackLayout.GestureRecognizers>
                                    <TapGestureRecognizer Command="{Binding Source={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type ContentPage}}, Path=BindingContext.EditItemCompositionCommand}"
                                                          CommandParameter="{Binding .}" />
                                </VerticalStackLayout.GestureRecognizers>
                                <VerticalStackLayout BindableLayout.ItemsSource="{Binding Components}" Spacing="0">
                                    <BindableLayout.ItemTemplate>
                                        <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:LineComponentViewModel">
                                            <Label Text="{Binding Text}" Style="{StaticResource TagText}" FontSize="11" LineBreakMode="TailTruncation" />
                                        </DataTemplate>
                                    </BindableLayout.ItemTemplate>
                                </VerticalStackLayout>
                            </VerticalStackLayout>
                        </VerticalStackLayout>

                        <!-- The unit price, and under it — only when something was actually overridden
                             — the price it was allowed to be. Tapping re-prices the line and nothing
                             else on the row does; the allowed price stays, so the difference survives
                             into the shift report's «Скидки» section. No reason is asked for — a reason
                             typed at the till becomes the first value anyone ever picks. Read off
                             OrderItem.ListPriceKopecks, which UpdateOrderAsync deliberately never
                             recomputes on an edit. -->
                        <VerticalStackLayout Grid.Column="1" Spacing="0" VerticalOptions="Center">
                            <Label Text="{Binding PriceText}" FontAttributes="Bold" FontSize="14"
                                   HorizontalTextAlignment="End" LineBreakMode="NoWrap"
                                   SemanticProperties.Description="{Binding PriceText, StringFormat='Цена {0}. Нажмите, чтобы изменить.'}">
                                <Label.GestureRecognizers>
                                    <TapGestureRecognizer Command="{Binding Source={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type ContentPage}}, Path=BindingContext.EditItemPriceCommand}"
                                                          CommandParameter="{Binding .}" />
                                </Label.GestureRecognizers>
                            </Label>
                            <Label Text="{Binding ListPriceText}" Style="{StaticResource SecondaryLabel}" FontSize="11"
                                   TextDecorations="Strikethrough" HorizontalTextAlignment="End"
                                   LineBreakMode="NoWrap"
                                   IsVisible="{Binding IsPriceOverridden}"
                                   SemanticProperties.Description="Разрешённая цена" />
                        </VerticalStackLayout>

                        <!-- The steppers and the delete button were 36dp, below the 44dp touch minimum
                             every other control in the app honours. Buttons, not a text field: Baymard
                             found 61% of sites get the quantity control wrong and, of the redesigns,
                             97% — dropdowns and text inputs producing a quantity of «21» instead of
                             «2». -->
                        <Grid Grid.Column="2" ColumnDefinitions="48,24,48" VerticalOptions="Center">
                            <Button Text="−" Padding="0" FontSize="18" HeightRequest="48" WidthRequest="48" MinimumHeightRequest="48" MinimumWidthRequest="48" SemanticProperties.Description="Убрать одну штуку" Command="{Binding Source={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type ContentPage}}, Path=BindingContext.DecreaseItemCommand}" CommandParameter="{Binding .}" IsEnabled="{Binding Source={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type ContentPage}}, Path=BindingContext.CanEdit}" />
                            <Label Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding Quantity}" HorizontalTextAlignment="Center" VerticalTextAlignment="Center" />
                            <Button Grid.Column="2" Text="+" Padding="0" FontSize="18" HeightRequest="48" WidthRequest="48" MinimumHeightRequest="48" MinimumWidthRequest="48" SemanticProperties.Description="Добавить ещё одну" Command="{Binding Source={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type ContentPage}}, Path=BindingContext.IncreaseItemCommand}" CommandParameter="{Binding .}" IsEnabled="{Binding Source={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type ContentPage}}, Path=BindingContext.CanEdit}" />
                        </Grid>

                        <!-- The whole line, in one tap. KEPT, and kept separate from the stepper on
                             purpose: stepping down to zero is a quantity edit, removing the line is a
                             deletion, and conflating them is how a mistap becomes an empty order. It is
                             the widest thing on the row and it is worth the width — the name is what
                             truncates, which is the correct thing to lose. -->
                        <Button Grid.Column="3" Text="Удалить" Style="{StaticResource DangerButton}" FontSize="12"
                                Padding="8,4" MinimumHeightRequest="44"
                                Command="{Binding Source={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type ContentPage}}, Path=BindingContext.RemoveItemCommand}"
                                CommandParameter="{Binding .}"
                                IsEnabled="{Binding Source={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type ContentPage}}, Path=BindingContext.CanEdit}" />
                    </Grid>
                </DataTemplate>
            </CollectionView.ItemTemplate>
        </CollectionView>
        <VerticalStackLayout Grid.Row="4" Spacing="8">
            <Picker Title="Добавить блюдо" ItemsSource="{Binding AvailableProducts}" ItemDisplayBinding="{Binding Name}" SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedProduct}" IsEnabled="{Binding CanEdit}" />
            <!-- The total used to sit BELOW the action buttons, right-aligned and clipped by the
                 tab bar — the most important number on an order-editing screen was the last thing
                 on it and the easiest to miss. It now heads the action block, above the
                 "Добавить блюдо" field, where the cart already puts it. -->
            <Label Text="{Binding TotalText, StringFormat='Итого: {0}'}" FontAttributes="Bold" FontSize="20" HorizontalTextAlignment="End" />
            <!-- Payment area: what was paid, what came back and what is left. The total is right
                 above, so this line carries the figures; the wording (not just the colour) carries
                 the state, and a refunded order says it was refunded rather than reading as unpaid.
                 -->
            <Label Text="{Binding PaymentSummary}" FontSize="13" FontAttributes="Bold" TextColor="{Binding PaymentColor}" LineBreakMode="WordWrap" />
            <Button Text="Принять оплату" Style="{StaticResource SecondaryButton}" MinimumHeightRequest="48" IsVisible="{Binding CanCollectPayment}" Command="{Binding CollectPaymentCommand}" SemanticProperties.Description="Принять оплату по заказу" />

            <!-- «Вернуть оплату» sits in the payment block, beside «Принять оплату», because it is
                 the same control facing the other way and an operator looking for it will look
                 there. Visible only on a Completed order that still holds money (CanRefundPayment):
                 the domain restricts refunds to finished sales, and the NET scalar is what makes the
                 button disappear once everything has been given back — so a fully refunded order
                 never offers a control that can only fail.
                 DangerButton rather than SecondaryButton because money leaves the till. The colour
                 is NOT the signal: the wording is («Вернуть», not «Оплата»), and the sheet it opens
                 is titled and captioned as a return as well. -->
            <Button Text="Вернуть оплату" Style="{StaticResource DangerButton}" MinimumHeightRequest="48" IsVisible="{Binding CanRefundPayment}" Command="{Binding RefundPaymentCommand}" SemanticProperties.Description="Вернуть часть оплаты по закрытому заказу" />

            <!-- «Дописать» — the phone and the promised time, added to an order that is ALREADY paid
                 for. The customer thinks of it after the money changes hands, which is the only time it
                 can happen: before payment the cart sheet is still open and already asks.
                 It lives in the payment block because that is where an operator looks once the money is
                 in, and because the two controls around it («Принять оплату», «Вернуть оплату») are the
                 other things one does to a settled order.
                 Visible only on a paid, non-voided order (CanAddContactDetails), and it is never
                 disabled for a counter-service order: the fulfilment question is asked INSIDE the sheet,
                 as a tick the operator makes, because a button that silently refuses to act would be worse
                 than one that opens and explains. -->
            <Button Text="Дописать телефон или время" Style="{StaticResource SecondaryButton}" MinimumHeightRequest="48" IsVisible="{Binding CanAddContactDetails}" Command="{Binding AddContactDetailsCommand}" SemanticProperties.Description="Дописать в оплаченный заказ телефон клиента или время выдачи" />

            <!-- The individual payments, one line each.

                 Two columns: the words on the left, the signed amount on the right. A refund row
                 used to render identically to a collection row — the same text for money in and for
                 money out — because the ledger is one table and IsRefund is a flag, not a shape.
                 Now the amount carries a minus sign, the text carries «возврат», and the amount is
                 tinted. The tint is the THIRD signal and never the only one: colour alone is refused
                 throughout this app, and a screen reader is given the word, not the colour.

                 The tint comes from PaymentLine.AmountColor rather than from the template, because a
                 DataTrigger's Setter takes a VALUE and not a binding expression: every token put in
                 one — plain {StaticResource Danger}, and then an AppThemeBinding inside the very
                 same Setter — resolved once at parse time and stayed light-themed in dark mode. It
                 went through both, and at 12pt bold this label owes 4.5:1, where it was getting
                 3.76:1. A bound property resolves against the theme that is actually running; see
                 its remarks for the numbers and for the one coupling it introduces. -->
            <VerticalStackLayout BindableLayout.ItemsSource="{Binding Payments}" Spacing="2">
                <BindableLayout.ItemTemplate>
                    <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:PaymentLine">
                        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8">
                            <Label Text="{Binding Text}" FontSize="12" Style="{StaticResource SecondaryLabel}" />
                            <Label Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding AmountText}" FontSize="12" FontAttributes="Bold"
                                   VerticalTextAlignment="Start" TextColor="{Binding AmountColor}" />
                        </Grid>
                    </DataTemplate>
                </BindableLayout.ItemTemplate>
            </VerticalStackLayout>

            <Button Text="Добавить в заказ" Command="{Binding AddProductCommand}" IsEnabled="{Binding CanEdit}" />
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,*" ColumnSpacing="8">
                <Button Text="Сохранить заказ" Command="{Binding SaveCommand}" IsEnabled="{Binding CanEdit}" SemanticProperties.Description="Сохранить изменения в этом заказе" />
                <!-- Was Command="{Binding CancelCommand}", which OrderDetailsViewModel does not
                     declare - it exposes BackCommand. The binding resolved to null, so this button
                     rendered disabled and "Назад" did nothing: the operator could not leave this
                     screen by the button at all, and BackCommand was bound nowhere, i.e. dead code.
                     x:DataType is what made the mismatch visible at build time. -->
                <Button Grid.Column="1" Text="Назад" Style="{StaticResource SecondaryButton}" Command="{Binding BackCommand}" />
            </Grid>

            <!-- The void. This page had no cancel control at all before, which left the natural
                 place to undo one specific order unable to undo anything: the operator had to go
                 and find the row on a different screen. Same three dialogs as the board's cancel,
                 in the same order and with the same per-row wording, so the two entry points cannot
                 drift apart on the phrasing that tells the operator money is leaving. CanCancel
                 keeps it off an already-cancelled order, where the domain would refuse it.

                 Last in the block on purpose: it is the most destructive thing on the page, and it
                 reads after everything the operator might have wanted instead. -->
            <Button Text="{Binding CancelText}" Style="{StaticResource DangerButton}" MinimumHeightRequest="48" IsVisible="{Binding CanCancel}" Command="{Binding CancelOrderCommand}" SemanticProperties.Description="{Binding CancelHint}" />
        </VerticalStackLayout>
    </Grid>
</ContentPage>